{"content_hash": "ba73b564edc2035e", "version_id": 609, "article_id": 61, "level_code": "ja_n2", "lang": "ja", "level": 4, "level_label": "N2 中高級", "title": "英国儿童癌症症状易被忽视、専門家が注意を呼びかけ", "title_zh": "英国儿童癌症症状容易被忽视，专家呼吁家长提高警惕", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "bbc_health", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwm237z77dgyo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1616408621507-88179d36a3a0?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8Mnx8Y2hpbGRob29kJTIwY2FuY2VyJTIwc3ltcHRvbXN8ZW58MHwwfHx8MTc5MTc2NTkwNnww&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Vitolda Klein on Unsplash", "author": "Vitolda Klein", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@little_klein?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/girl-in-white-and-blue-floral-dress-lying-on-white-fur-textile-ebiDpT4pYdw?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(195, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(233, 46%, 48%)", "label": "HEALTH", "seed": "2c5b225ed52f"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-12", "word_count": 285, "reading_minutes": 0.9, "lead": "英国では毎年約1900人の子どもががんと診断される。しかし、症状を正しく認識できると自信を持つ親は3割に満たない。専門家は、持続する症状の組み合わせに注意するよう呼びかけている。", "lead_zh": "英国每年约有1900名儿童被诊断出癌症。然而，只有不到三成的家长有信心能识别出相关症状。专家呼吁家长留意持续出现的症状组合。", "preview": "英国では15歳未満の子どもの病死原因の首位はがんだが、まれな病気である。13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは昨年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母親のジェマさんによると、初期症状は疲労感や軽い運動後の息切れで、単なる体力不足や学校後の疲れだと思われた。しかし、あざが現れてから10日以", "preview_zh": "在英国，癌症是15岁以下儿童病死原因之首，但属罕见病。13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断为急性髓系白血病。据母亲杰玛说，初期症状是疲劳和轻微运动后气喘，被误认为体力不足或放学后的疲惫。然而，出现淤青后10天内就确诊了白血病。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 589, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/61/ja_n2.mp3", "duration": 61.987, "size_bytes": 375696, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 186, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@f76eeb6/content/data/versions/609.json", "paragraphs": ["英国では15歳未満の子どもの病死原因の首位はがんだが、まれな病気である。13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは昨年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母親のジェマさんによると、初期症状は疲労感や軽い運動後の息切れで、単なる体力不足や学校後の疲れだと思われた。しかし、あざが現れてから10日以内に白血病と確定した。", "小児腫瘍科医のボブ・フィリップス教授は、親が注意すべきは「持続し、悪化し、大きくなる症状の組み合わせ」だと述べる。具体的には、極度の疲労感、青白い顔色、原因不明のあざ、大きく成長するしこりなどである。子どものがんは成長が早いため、大人より急速に進行する。", "フィリップス氏らは、診断を早めるための全国キャンペーンを進めている。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["在英国，癌症是15岁以下儿童病死原因之首，但属罕见病。13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断为急性髓系白血病。据母亲杰玛说，初期症状是疲劳和轻微运动后气喘，被误认为体力不足或放学后的疲惫。然而，出现淤青后10天内就确诊了白血病。", "儿科肿瘤医生鲍勃·菲利普斯教授指出，家长应注意的是“持续、恶化、变大的症状组合”。具体包括极度疲劳、面色苍白、不明原因的淤青、不断长大的肿块等。由于儿童生长快，癌症比成人进展更迅速。", "菲利普斯等人正在推进一项全国性运动，以加快诊断。针对专业人员提出了“三法则”：即出现三种持续症状、就诊三次、或不明症状持续三周，就应考虑转诊专科医生。"], "body": "英国では15歳未満の子どもの病死原因の首位はがんだが、まれな病気である。13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは昨年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母親のジェマさんによると、初期症状は疲労感や軽い運動後の息切れで、単なる体力不足や学校後の疲れだと思われた。しかし、あざが現れてから10日以内に白血病と確定した。\n\n小児腫瘍科医のボブ・フィリップス教授は、親が注意すべきは「持続し、悪化し、大きくなる症状の組み合わせ」だと述べる。具体的には、極度の疲労感、青白い顔色、原因不明のあざ、大きく成長するしこりなどである。子どものがんは成長が早いため、大人より急速に進行する。\n\nフィリップス氏らは、診断を早めるための全国キャンペーンを進めている。", "body_zh": "在英国，癌症是15岁以下儿童病死原因之首，但属罕见病。13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断为急性髓系白血病。据母亲杰玛说，初期症状是疲劳和轻微运动后气喘，被误认为体力不足或放学后的疲惫。然而，出现淤青后10天内就确诊了白血病。\n\n儿科肿瘤医生鲍勃·菲利普斯教授指出，家长应注意的是“持续、恶化、变大的症状组合”。具体包括极度疲劳、面色苍白、不明原因的淤青、不断长大的肿块等。由于儿童生长快，癌症比成人进展更迅速。\n\n菲利普斯等人正在推进一项全国性运动，以加快诊断。针对专业人员提出了“三法则”：即出现三种持续症状、就诊三次、或不明症状持续三周，就应考虑转诊专科医生。", "text": "英国では15歳未満の子どもの病死原因の首位はがんだが、まれな病気である。13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは昨年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母親のジェマさんによると、初期症状は疲労感や軽い運動後の息切れで、単なる体力不足や学校後の疲れだと思われた。しかし、あざが現れてから10日以内に白血病と確定した。 小児腫瘍科医のボブ・フィリップス教授は、親が注意すべきは「持続し、悪化し、大きくなる症状の組み合わせ」だと述べる。具体的には、極度の疲労感、青白い顔色、原因不明のあざ、大きく成長するしこりなどである。子どものがんは成長が早いため、大人より急速に進行する。 フィリップス氏らは、診断を早めるための全国キャンペーンを進めている。", "vocab": [{"word": "診断", "pos": "noun", "zh": "诊断", "note": "常用于医疗语境", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "症状", "pos": "noun", "zh": "症状", "note": "描述疾病表现", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "疲労感", "pos": "noun", "zh": "疲劳感", "note": "身体疲倦的感觉", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "あざ", "pos": "noun", "zh": "淤青", "note": "皮肤上的紫斑", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "持続する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "持续", "note": "自动词，表示状态延续", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "悪化する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "恶化", "note": "病情或状况变差", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "しこり", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肿块", "note": "身体上的硬块", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "小児", "pos": "noun", "zh": "儿童", "note": "医学用语，指小儿", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "腫瘍", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肿瘤", "note": "医学用语", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "専門医", "pos": "noun", "zh": "专科医生", "note": "特定领域的医生", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "紹介", "pos": "noun", "zh": "转诊；介绍", "note": "医疗中指转介", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "生存率", "pos": "noun", "zh": "生存率", "note": "存活比例", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "寛解", "pos": "noun", "zh": "缓解", "note": "症状暂时消失", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "認知度", "pos": "noun", "zh": "认知度", "note": "公众了解程度", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for", "summary_original": "Although rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK.", "body_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for\nBy\nCamilla Horrox\nHealth reporter\nPublished\n1 hour ago\nAlthough rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK - yet just under a third of parents say they feel confident recognising the symptoms.\nAbout 1,900 children aged 0-14 are diagnosed with cancer each year, according to Cancer Research UK.\nThirteen-year-old Amelia Carroll, who is now in remission, was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) last year.\nHer mum Gemma recalls the initial symptoms being easy to overlook.\nThere was the tiredness, she says, and being out of breath after mild exercise but both were put down to being unfit and just post-school fatigue.\nIt was only when Amelia started presenting with bruises Gemma knew something wasn't right - within 10 days Amelia got her leukaemia diagnosis.\nWhat are the symptoms to look out for?\nProf Bob Phillips, a paediatric oncologist, says it's key for parents to look out for clusters of symptoms that may be persisting, getting worse or bigger.\nConsider contacting a doctor over the following:\nExtreme tiredness\nLooking really pale\nBruises that you can't explain\nLumps and bumps that are getting bigger or not going away and growing over time\nCancer in children develops much more quickly than in adults - effectively due to them growing.\nExperts like Phillips at CCLG, the Children and Young People's Cancer Association, are urging people to follow a national campaign aiming to raise awareness and improve diagnosis times.\nIt includes a rule-of-three for professionals: If a child has had three persistent symptoms, or has visited their doctor three times, or has had three weeks of unexplained symptoms consider making a referral.\nAlthough, it's important to note survival rates have risen significantly since the 1970s, with eight out of 10 children surviving beyond five to 10 years or more after diagnosis.\nFor Amelia, her cancer journey began with a single red bruise that came out of nowhere, says Gemma.\nThree days later, she had bruises down both of her shins on both legs, which were sore and raised.\n\"We'd gone to the opticians for a routine appointment and they noticed that she'd got some burst blood vessels but they weren't sure why,\" she adds.\nWithin 10 days, doctors had confirmed what type of leukaemia Amelia had and she was admitted to hospital to begin treatment.\n\"On the day that they confirmed what she had, I sat her down and she said to me 'have I got cancer?' and I said yes you have. She asked me if she was going to die.\"\n\"I said no, they're doing everything they can, they've got a plan in place and the treatment is going to do everything it can to beat the cancer.\"\nAmelia is currently in remission, undergoing regular bone marrow and blood tests every three months, and has returned to school.\nPhillips says survival rates for childhood cancer in the UK are over 80% but that even quicker diagnosis would lead to less intensive treatments.\n'Raising awareness is his legacy'\nAround the UK, about half the children diagnosed with cancer get their diagnosis within two weeks, he says.\n\"It's pretty quick but we want the other half to not have the wait of 16, 18, 20, 25 weeks from those first symptoms to get a diagnosis.\"\nCancer Research UK data shows that there are more than 400,000 new cases of cancer diagnosed every year in the UK, with only 54% being told within the 28-day NHS target window, according to the British Medical Journal (BMJ).\nAn additional 2,300 teenagers and young adults (aged 15–24) are diagnosed each year.\nLynsey Swinscoe is mum to Jake, who died of soft tissue cancer aged 13. Jake was diagnosed with stage three, fusion positive alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma in July 2023.\nBefore his diagnosis, Jake's initial symptoms of a stuffy nose and watery eyes were put down to hayfever, and falling asleep after school was thought to be just end-of-term tiredness.\n\"It was only when he started to get a swelling across the bridge of his nose that we thought right, we need to take him to the GP,\" Lynsey says.\n\"But at that point we didn't really think the fatigue and hayfever symptoms were related.\"\nA mass was found in Jake's nose and nasal cavity and he was diagnosed with soft tissue sarcoma. He completed nine rounds of chemotherapy and proton beam radiation.\nAfter suffering seizures and feeling very unwell from the chemotherapy, Jake opted out of continuing treatment.\nHe passed away peacefully at home on 26 April 2024, just two weeks before his 14th birthday.\n\"I think the main thing with Jake is he was not scared to die. We had lots of conversations about dying and what it might feel like to die and the only thing that he was sort of scared about was, 'are all the people going to remember me? Is everyone just going to forget who I am?'\n\"That's why I'm doing this, to make sure that people know who Jake is, to make sure people know how incredible he was. And so with that comes the raising awareness, which I think, that's his legacy.\"\nRelated topics\nLeukaemia\nCancer\nChild health\nNHS\nHealth\nChildhood cancer\nMore on this story\nMother writes book to help children with cancer\nPublished\n4 October\nGirl, 7, rings bell to mark end of cancer treatment\nPublished\n1 October\n'This doesn't happen to us' - mum on child's cancer\nPublished\n12 September", "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T00:45:05.386127", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "英国", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.6, "sm": 125, "em": 600, "cs": 0, "ce": 2, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "で", "s": 0.613, "e": 0.713, "sm": 612, "em": 712, "cs": 2, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "は", "s": 0.725, "e": 0.9, "sm": 725, "em": 899, "cs": 3, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "15", "s": 0.912, "e": 1.337, "sm": 912, "em": 1337, "cs": 4, "ce": 6, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "歳", "s": 1.337, "e": 1.55, "sm": 1337, "em": 1549, "cs": 6, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "未満", "s": 1.562, "e": 1.887, "sm": 1562, "em": 1887, "cs": 7, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "の", "s": 1.9, "e": 2.038, "sm": 1900, "em": 2037, "cs": 9, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "子ども", "s": 2.05, "e": 2.412, "sm": 2050, "em": 2412, "cs": 10, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "の", "s": 2.425, "e": 2.537, "sm": 2425, "em": 2537, "cs": 13, "ce": 14, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "病死", "s": 2.55, "e": 2.938, "sm": 2550, "em": 2937, "cs": 14, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "原因", "s": 2.95, "e": 3.35, "sm": 2950, "em": 3350, "cs": 16, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "の", "s": 3.362, "e": 3.475, "sm": 3362, "em": 3474, "cs": 18, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "首位", "s": 3.487, "e": 3.762, "sm": 3487, "em": 3762, "cs": 19, "ce": 21, "si": 0}, {"i": 13, "w": "は", "s": 3.775, "e": 3.962, "sm": 3775, "em": 3962, "cs": 21, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 14